<!-- When the form from views/notes/new.html.erb submits,
NotesController#create looks for a view matching
create.turbo_stream.erb. So we make that file here. -->

<!-- What this code does: Here Turbo replaces the form frame
from views/notes/new.html.erb with the freshly
updated notes/index view containing the newly created note -->

<!-- We replace the #notice_board_content with the updated index board view -->
<turbo-stream action="replace" target="notice_board_content">
  <template>
    <%= render template: "notes/index", formats: [:html] %> <!-- See note below -->
  </template>
</turbo-stream>

<!-- When Rails processes 'create.turbo_stream.erb',
it searches for a template named 'notes/index' that explicitly
supports the :turbo_stream format.

But the 'app/views/notes/index.html.erb' file is saved with
the .html.erb extension.

Because Rails is currently inside a Turbo Stream request context,
it skips index.html.erb (it can't naturally read this file)
and then complains that 'notes/index.turbo_stream.erb' doesn't exist.

The solution: Add 'formats: [:html]' to the template -->

<!-- Changes to NotesController#create -->

<!-- We need to re-fetch @notes in NotesController#create,
because when the form submits, it hits NotesController#create.
Inside that create action, Rails instantiated @note = @category.notes.build(...),
but it no longer has access to @notes.

So when create.turbo_stream.erb tries to render template: "notes/index",
Rails evaluates notes/index.html.erb inside the context of
NotesController#create where @notes is nil.a
This triggers an 'undefined method 'any?' for nil' error. -->
